WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.274
This video is about the agents at the frontier right now: agents that run for hours, agents that write software, and agents that operate a computer through the screen.

2
00:00:09.324 --> 00:00:12.490
You have used at least one of them this term.

3
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.670
The point here is what they change for everything this course has taught.

4
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.835
Start with long-running agents.

5
00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:30.560
A task that takes a model hours or days cannot fit one context window, so the work is split into sessions, and each session starts with no memory of the last one.

6
00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:37.155
Anthropic's November 2025 write-up of harnesses for this kind of agent is a good model of the engineering.

7
00:00:37.205 --> 00:00:53.277
Continuity lives in the harness: a progress file the agent reads first and updates last, a task list with items marked done, clean checkpoints at the end of each session, and tests that define what done means, so the agent cannot simply declare victory.

8
00:00:53.327 --> 00:01:00.238
Notice that all of it is week one's harness and week four's evaluation, applied at a longer time scale.

9
00:01:00.988 --> 00:01:06.215
Coding agents are agents whose tools are a file system, a shell and a test runner.

10
00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:13.800
You are allowed to use them in this course, and the oral trace check exists so that you can explain what they did.

11
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:17.097
They work unusually well, and it is worth asking why.

12
00:01:17.147 --> 00:01:20.725
Code has tests, so the agent can check its own work.

13
00:01:20.775 --> 00:01:24.353
Errors are cheap inside a sandbox, and version control undoes mistakes.

14
00:01:24.403 --> 00:01:26.991
Most business domains have none of those advantages.

15
00:01:27.041 --> 00:01:32.597
There is no test suite for a customer email, and there is no undo for a payment.

16
00:01:32.647 --> 00:01:40.842
When someone shows you a coding-agent result and promises the same for your domain, ask what plays the role of the test and the undo.

17
00:01:41.591 --> 00:01:45.751
Computer-use agents look at the screen, move the mouse and type.

18
00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:54.169
They matter because much of the work in organizations happens in systems with no API: legacy applications and vendor portals.

19
00:01:54.219 --> 00:02:04.500
They are slow and expensive, since every step costs a screenshot's worth of tokens, and every screen is untrusted content, so the lethal trifecta applies in full.

20
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:09.092
Pass rates on long tasks are still well below human, and improving.

21
00:02:09.142 --> 00:02:14.831
For a capstone, a computer-use step belongs behind a gate, if it belongs at all.

22
00:02:15.581 --> 00:02:16.937
What stays the same?

23
00:02:16.987 --> 00:02:18.343
Everything on this list.

24
00:02:18.393 --> 00:02:20.803
The loop, the harness and the trace.

25
00:02:20.853 --> 00:02:24.669
Golden sets and pass^k, now over longer tasks.

26
00:02:24.719 --> 00:02:27.130
The trifecta, now with more untrusted input.

27
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.941
Cost per task, now larger and more variable.

28
00:02:29.991 --> 00:02:32.050
And a person who owns it.

29
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.862
The frontier moves the scale of each question.

30
00:02:34.912 --> 00:02:36.971
It has not changed the questions.

31
00:02:37.021 --> 00:02:43.297
The last video asks how far the capability has actually come, using the benchmarks rather than the demos.
